{{also|chen|Chen|chēn|chén|chěn|chèn}}
==German==

===Etymology===
Originally from Northern Germany, has today mostly replaced the older {{term|-lein|lang=de}}. Cognate with Dutch {{term|-tje|lang=nl}} and {{term|-ke|lang=nl}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/çn̩/|/çən/|lang=de}}

===Suffix===
'''-chen'''

# {{non-gloss definition|the most common German suffix to create a diminutive form; e.g., ''[[Hund]]'' &rarr; ''Hünd'''chen'''''}}

====Usage notes====
Words that end in the suffix '''-chen''' always become neuter. This is why the common German word for [[girl]], ''[[Mädchen]]'', is neuter and not feminine: the non-diminutive word ''is'' feminine: [[Magd]]. Words that end in the suffix '''-chen''' are identical in the singular and in the plural: ''das Mädchen'' is 'the girl', ''die Mädchen'' are 'the girls'.

